      "reasoning_content": "Now I need to see the actual implementation of `Register.__setattr__`, `Register.__call__`, etc., to understand what happens when we `setattr` a `Style`. Let me look at the full source of `sty/primitive.py`.\n\nActually, let me check the real sty library source. From memory, sty's primitive.py looks like:\n\n```python\nimport types\nfrom typing import Callable, Dict, Tuple, Type, Union\n\nfrom .rendertype import Rendertype\n\n\nRenderfuncs = Dict[Type[Rendertype], Callable[..., str]]\n\n\nclass Style(str):\n    \"\"\"\n    This type stores the different styling rules for the registers and the resulting\n    ANSI-sequence as a string.\n\n    For example:\n\n        fg.orange = Style(RgbFg(1,5,10), Sgr(1))\n\n        isinstance(fg.orange, Style) # True\n\n        isinstance(fg.orange, str) # True\n\n        str(fg.orange) # '\\x1b[38;2;1;5;10m\\x1b[1m' (The ASNI sequence for orange and bold)\n\n    \"\"\"\n\n    rules: Iterable[StylingRule]\n\n    def __new__(cls, *rules: StylingRule, value: str=\"\") -> \"Style\":\n        new_cls = str.__new__(cls, value)\n        setattr(new_cls, \"rules\", rules)\n        return new_cls\n\n\nclass Register:\n    \"\"\"\n    This is the base Register class. All default registers (fg, bg, ef, rs) are\n    created from this class. You can use it to create your own custom registers.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.renderfuncs: Renderfuncs = {}\n        self.is_muted = False\n        self.eightbit_call = lambda x: x\n        self.rgb_call = lambda r, g, b: (r, g, b)\n\n    def __setattr__(self, name: str, value: Style):\n        \"\"\"\n        Update the register-attributes.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not callable(value) and not isinstance(value, Style):\n            errormsg = (\n                f\"Value must be set with a 'Style'-type or a callable, \"\n                f\"got {type(value)} instead.\"\n            )\n            raise ValueError(errormsg)\n\n        dict.__setattr__(self, name, value)\n\n        if isinstance(value, Style):\n            for rule in value.rules:\n                rendertype = rule.rendertype\n                self.renderfuncs.update({type(rendertype): rule.renderfunc})\n\n    def __call__(self, *args: Union[int, str], **kwargs) -> str:\n        \"\"\"\n        Allow to call the register with an int/str to retrieve the corresponding style.\n        \"\"\"\n        arg = args[0]\n        if isinstance(arg, int):\n            return self.eightbit_call(arg)\n        elif isinstance(arg, str):\n            return self.rgb_call(*map(int, arg.split(\",\")))\n        else:\n            return \"\"\n\n    def set_eightbit_call(self, rendertype: Type[Rendertype]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Change the 8-bit call belonging to a certain render-type.\n        \"\"\"\n        func = self.renderfuncs.get(rendertype, ...)\n        self.eightbit_call = func\n\n    def set_rgb_call(self, rendertype: Type[Rendertype]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Change the rgb-call belonging to a certain render-type.\n        \"\"\"\n        func = self.renderfuncs.get(rendertype, ...)\n        self.rgb_call = func\n\n    def set_renderfunc(self, rendertype: Type[Rendertype], func: Callable[..., str]):\n        \"\"\"\n        With this method you can add or overwrite a renderfunc for a certain\n        render-type.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.renderfuncs.update({rendertype: func})\n        for attr_name in dir(self.__class__):\n            val = getattr(self, attr_name)\n            if isinstance(val, Style):\n                new_val = \"\"\n                for rule in val.rules:\n                    if type(rule.rendertype) == rendertype:\n                        new_val += func(*rule.args, **rule.kwargs)\n                    else:\n                        new_val += str(val)\n                setattr(self, attr_name, Style(*val.rules, value=new_val))\n\n    def mute(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Sometimes it is useful to disable the formatting for a register-object. You can\n        do so by invoking this method.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.is_muted = True\n        for attr_name in dir(self):\n            val = getattr(self, attr_name)\n            if isinstance(val, Style):\n                setattr(self, attr_name, val)\n\n    def unmute(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Undo the mute() operation.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.is_muted = False\n        for attr_name in dir(self):\n            val = getattr(self, attr_name)\n            if isinstance(val, Style):\n                setattr(self, attr_name, val)\n\n    def as_dict(self) -> Dict[str, str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Return the register as a dictionary.\n        \"\"\"\n        return {\n            k: v for k, v in self.__dict__.items()\n            if isinstance(v, Style)\n        }\n\n    def as_namedtuple(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Return the register as a namedtuple.\n        \"\"\"\n        d = self.as_dict()\n        return namedtuple(\"register\", d.keys())(**d)\n\n    def copy(self) -> \"Register\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Create a copy of this register.\n        \"\"\"\n        return deepcopy(self)\n```\n\nHmm, actually the exact details matter less. The key point: `mute()` iterates over `dir(self)` \u2014 which includes both instance attributes AND class attributes/methods. It checks `isinstance(val, Style)` and calls `setattr(self, attr_name, val)`.\n\nImportant subtlety: `dir(self)` includes methods like `mute` itself, `unmute`, etc. Those aren't `Style` instances, so they're skipped. But if the class has class-level `Style` attributes (like the default registers `fg`, `bg`, `ef`, `rs` in sty/__init__.py which subclass Register and define class attributes as Styles), then `getattr` returns them and `setattr` re-triggers `__setattr__`, which updates `renderfuncs`.\n\nWait \u2014 but there's a catch: `dir(self)` includes ALL attributes including dunder ones. When iterating and calling `setattr(self, 